fuxi: 0109 -- read the EPSS perf state, so the cores' clock stops being a guess

PMCCNTR reads 0 because the PMU is trapped at EL2 (0105 reports MIDR and CNTFRQ
but "clock unreadable"), and the shim cannot fix that from EL1.  The EPSS block
does not need the PMU at all.

sm8550.dtsi puts three freq domains at 0x17d91000 / 0x17d92000 / 0x17d93000
("freq-domain0/1/2" = A510 x3, A715+A710 x4, X3), and
drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c gives the epss layout: freq LUT at 0x100 (u32
per entry, lval in bits 7:0, xo in bits 31:30, freq_kHz = 19200 * lval), the
requested perf state at 0x320, and the current vote in XO units at 0x20.

So the report is direct: perf_state is the index, LUT[index] is the clock the
cluster is at right now, and printing the LUT range next to it says whether that
index is the bottom, the top, or neither.  Since nothing in this port ever writes
perf_state, this number is exactly the OPP the bootloader left behind -- which is
the whole "is it boosting" question, answered instead of argued.

The cluster names come from the same DT: cpu0-2 cortex-a510, cpu3-4 cortex-a715,
cpu5-6 cortex-a710, cpu7 cortex-x3, which matches the mpidr 0x000..0x700 the SMP
bring-up prints.

Harness: applied=108 skipped=0.
